  <p class="kicker">Chapter proposal <span class="badge">draft v1 · 20 October 2026</span></p>
  <h1>From Papers to Progress, with Agents: Governing AI-Assisted Empirical Software Engineering Research</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    For the Springer edited volume <em>Using Artificial Intelligence in
    Empirical Software Engineering Research</em> (eds. Mäntylä, Mendez, Franch,
    Kalinowski). The call asks for a one-page proposal covering five items; they
    are reproduced here in that order.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Working draft under review by the co-authors ahead of the 23 October 2026
    deadline. Wording, title and scope may change before submission.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Intended authors</h2>
  <p>
    Jason Cusati and Chris Brown, Department of Computer Science, Virginia
    Tech, Blacksburg, VA, USA.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Submission category</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Category 2 — Cross-Cutting Topics, Foundations, and Ethics</strong>:
    methodological rigor, validity and reliability; human-in-the-loop
    frameworks; reproducibility, transparency and open science; pipeline
    design. Overlaps Category 1 (artifact and replication-package generation).
  </p>

  <h2>3. General goals and alignment with the target audience</h2>
  <p>
    AI is no longer a tool a researcher calls at one step of a study; in
    empirical software engineering it now plans, retrieves, extracts and
    critiques, with decisions delegated to it along the way <Fragment set:html={c("gomes2026")} />.
    Current guidance asks authors to disclose models and prompts, keep session
    traces and validate AI output by hand <Fragment set:html={c("baltes2025")} />,
    and stage-specific frameworks now govern literature screening <Fragment set:html={c("kitchenham2026")} />{" "}
    and repository mining <Fragment set:html={c("demartino2025")} />. What no
    standard yet specifies is the research <em>process</em> as a governed
    object: which decisions were delegated to AI and under what authority,
    which intermediate artifacts they produced, and which human checks
    accepted them, recorded as reviewable provenance across the whole study.
    This is the loss of provenance already known to limit knowledge
    accumulation in software engineering <Fragment set:html={c("cusati2026")} />,
    now inside the study and at machine speed — and the same agents can make
    the remedy affordable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter operationalizes four published principles for research
    artifacts (structured, inspectable, long-lived, governed) <Fragment set:html={c("cusati2026")} />{" "}
    for AI-assisted empirical research: agents work under bounded authority,
    claims are recorded as typed entities, decisions leave durable provenance,
    and reproducibility is built in as executable checks. It is written for PhD
    students and early-career researchers who already use agents and need to
    know what to record and where human judgment must stay; for reviewers who
    need criteria for assessing agent-assisted studies; and for educators, who
    get a framework and worked examples to assign alongside existing methods
    guidance. It is grounded in working tooling and two cases from our own
    practice: an experience report and reference implementation, not a
    validated method.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Outline of chapter content</h2>
  <ol class="outline">
    <li><strong>From AI tool to co-researcher.</strong> Why agentic AI moves the methodological unit from prompt–output pairs to workflows, and what existing reporting guidance does not cover.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure modes of ungoverned AI-assisted research.</strong> Hidden delegated decisions, prompt and model drift, circular validation, non-durable context, schema choices that distort measured results, unrecorded human interventions — each tied to its validity threat.</li>
    <li><strong>A governance framework for agentic empirical research.</strong> The four principles as design patterns: bounded agent authority with explicit roles; typed claims (existence proof, method claim, general claim) and the baseline each requires; durable provenance of decisions; human semantic gates with stated criteria and placement.</li>
    <li><strong>Engineering reproducibility.</strong> Versioned agent definitions and prompts, a control plane separate from research artifacts, executable checks in CI, FAIR packaging.</li>
    <li><strong>Two worked cases.</strong> A literature study of agentic software engineering (a separate 268-record, schema-constrained store with adversarial verification) and a time-boxed empirical study in which a claims register, pre-registered thresholds and a human re-derivation ledger gated every agent-drafted result — including the lesson that recorded provenance does not govern by itself: a new agent session used the record reliably only when the protocol for loading it was itself governed.</li>
    <li><strong>Adoption guide and research agenda.</strong> A minimum governed workflow, a reporting checklist for agent-assisted studies, and open questions: how much process provenance is enough, measuring generator–evaluator independence, model drift in longitudinal studies.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Readers leave with a vocabulary for what agents may decide, a checklist for what to record, and installable templates.</p>

  <h2>5. Intended supplementary material</h2>
  <p>
    A FAIR-archived package (Zenodo, MIT/CC-BY): our open-source plugins{" "}
    <code>agentic-governance</code> and <code>agentic-research</code>{" "}(agent
    role contracts, decision-record, claims-register and verification-ledger
    templates, conformance-check scripts); the prompts and agent definitions
    used in both cases; the 219-record, independently verified{" "}
    <a href={track}>source dataset behind this proposal</a>; and a worked example
    repository showing the full lifecycle.
  </p>

